WHY THIS ONE, recorded because the choice is load-bearing rather than clerical.

  The ask this project makes of a stranger is: REIMPLEMENT THIS AND SEE WHETHER
  YOU GET THE SAME ROOT. Apache-2.0 grants patent rights explicitly, and a
  standards body evaluating a mechanism for reimplementation reads MIT's silence
  on patents as an unpriced risk. Its requirement that changed files be marked
  is also, in a different medium, what conformance already does.

  AGPL-3.0 was the closer philosophical fit -- every node's ethics gate readable
  by the people it judges -- and was rejected because it would have made this
  repository's own documents false. Every outreach draft promises that forking
  needs no permission and creates no obligation in either direction, and
  GOVERNANCE.md VI promises a sovereign fork under its own law. Copyleft creates
  an obligation. The promise was made in writing before the licence was chosen,
  so the licence was chosen to keep it.

  Until 2026-08-31 this file read LICENCE NOT YET CHOSEN, which under the Berne
  Convention means all rights reserved -- while six documents described the work
  as public domain and every letter asked a reader to clone it and run it. That
  contradiction was found by adversarial review of the OUTREACH, not by reading
  the licence, and it would have been found first by whoever opened this file
  before running anything.

